2. Market analysis and positioning
Fluxora should not try to beat payment giants. It should be the household money layer that sits on top of UPI: track, budget, split with flatmates, and settle through the UPI app people already use.
What the evidence says
• Finance app downloads in India are concentrated: PhonePe alone had 102.2M downloads in the year to April 2026, and the top five apps hold over a quarter of top-500 finance downloads (AppTweak). Competing on payments is a losing fight.
• Pure personal-finance software is a small market: about USD 42.5M in 2024, forecast to reach USD 63.6M by 2033 (IMARC). Growth will come from apps that are AI-assisted and mobile-first, so Fluxora must win on experience, not on market size alone.
• Splitwise's free plan caps daily expenses, which pushed Indian users to alternatives such as Tricount, Splid, Settle Up and Google Pay (Tricount comparison, News18 via ITVoice). Many of those only record debts and settle outside the app.
• Account Aggregator (AA) is now mature: 2.61 billion accounts were enabled for data sharing and 252.9 million users had linked accounts by 31 December 2025 (HyperVerge). Automatic bank data is the next big expectation, but it needs licensing steps (see section 5).
