Apply these rules everywhere:
1. One primary action per screen. Secondary actions go in an overflow menu or a bottom sheet.
2. Smart defaults. Date defaults to today, split defaults to equal, currency is INR, category is suggested from the last similar entry. Users change defaults only when they need to.
3. Hide what is not relevant yet. No roommate card until a roommate is connected. No budget card until a budget exists. Show a short empty state that teaches the first step.
4. Lists show 5, then "See all". Dashboard shows 5 recent transactions, 3 budget bars, 1 goal.
5. Advanced behind "More options". EXACT, PERCENTAGE and SHARES split modes, recurring rules, custom categories and CSV tools are not on the first screen.
6. Dynamic Island shows one thing at a time, only when the user can act on it (for example "Food budget at 85%"). Nothing to act on means it stays quiet.
7. Notifications are limited and grouped. At most one per category per day unless the user acts. Daily insight is one notification.
8. AI answers lead with a 2-sentence summary. A "Why this?" control expands the reasoning and the numbers used.
9. Errors are human. Show "Couldn't save. Check your connection and try again." Technical text and a support code sit behind a "Details" control.
10. Never show internal data. No document IDs, UIDs, raw timestamps, status enums or stack traces. Phone numbers appear masked (+91 98••••••10) except where the user is entering their own.
11. Onboarding is 3 screens maximum. Ask for notification, biometric and contact permissions in context, at the moment they are needed, with one line of why.
12. A Simple / Advanced switch in Settings. Simple is the default. Advanced reveals analytics, import tools and developer-style options.
13. Amount masking (section 10) is the user-facing privacy layer on top of these rules.
B. What the code exposes (encapsulation)
1. Feature modules expose a tiny API: one route/entry composable, one or two use-case interfaces, and read-only domain models. Everything else is Kotlin internal or private.
2. Repositories are interfaces in the domain layer. Firestore implementations are internal to the data layer and never imported by UI code.
3. Firestore DTOs never leave the data layer. Map to domain models at the boundary, then to display-ready UI state.
4. Money is a value class wrapping minor units. UI and ViewModels cannot do raw Long arithmetic on money; only FinancialEngine can.
5. One immutable UiState per screen plus one onEvent(...) function. Composables receive display strings, not domain objects.
6. Privileged logic lives behind narrow callable functions that return only the fields the caller needs, never whole documents.
7. AI receives aggregates, not records (section 9).
8. Secrets stay out of the app. No API keys in source, resources or BuildConfig.
9. Modules cannot reach into each other. A Gradle dependency rule or a lint check fails the build if a feature module imports another feature's internal code.
C. How the coding agent reports to you
Short answers by default: what was done, what passed, what is blocked, and the next step. Full detail goes into FLUXORA_IMPLEMENTATION_STATUS.md, not into chat.
