7. Core finance features
Build the "core loop" first: open the app, add a transaction in under 10 seconds, and see the dashboard update.
Money engine
FinancialEngine is the only place that does money arithmetic. It uses integer paise (Money value class), never Float or Double.
• formatIndianCurrency(money, showSign, style) uses lakh and crore grouping. Example: 12,345,678 paise formats as INR 1,23,456.78; 3,905,000 paise as INR 39,050.00. Support a symbol style (₹) for display and the INR prefix style for exports.
• Safe add, subtract and multiply with overflow detection (Math.addExact style); input parsing that rejects more than 2 decimals; negative amounts shown with a sign only when asked.
• Deterministic split allocation: shares are computed in paise, then the leftover paise are given one each to participants in a fixed order (for example sorted by uid). The shares always sum exactly to the total.
Transactions
• Types INCOME and EXPENSE. amountMinor in 1..MAX_AMOUNT_MINOR. DateValidator.validateTransactionDate allows today and past dates and rejects future dates, using the device's time zone (default Asia/Kolkata).
• Add, edit, delete, search, date filter, category filter.
• The client generates the document id (UUID). Retrying a save writes the same document, so there are no duplicates.
• Quick-add: a bottom sheet with a number pad, category chips (last-used first) and a Save button. Note, date and account are under "More".
Categories
Twelve defaults: Housing, Food & Dining, Groceries, Transportation, Education, Shopping, Entertainment, Health, Bills & Utilities, Travel, Subscriptions, Other. Defaults are global app constants that cannot be edited or deleted. Custom categories are per-user; names are trimmed, 1 to 30 characters, and unique ignoring case across defaults and custom ones. Deleting a custom category moves its transactions to "Other" after confirmation.
Budgets and savings goals
• Monthly category budgets. Usage = sum of that month's EXPENSE transactions in the category. Alerts at 80% (warning) and 100% (exceeded), each at most once per budget, per month and per threshold. Editing or deleting a transaction recalculates usage; if usage falls back below a threshold the alert can fire again later.
• Savings goals with milestones at 25, 50, 75 and 100%. Record the highest milestone already notified; a reversed contribution that drops below a milestone resets it. Contributions are tracked separately from expenses.
Recurring bills
Weekly, monthly and yearly. Month-end rule: a bill due on the 31st falls on the last day of shorter months; a 29 February yearly bill falls on 28 February in non-leap years.
markBillAsPaid runs as one Firestore transaction (or a callable function) that:
1. Checks that billPayments/{billId_dueDate} does not exist; if it does, returns the earlier result without writing anything.
2. Creates the expense with a deterministic id built from the bill and due date.
3. Creates the billPayments record.
4. Advances nextDueDate.
A 10-second debounce in the UI is kept as a courtesy, but the deterministic ids are the real protection against duplicates.
