9. AI financial advisor
Use Firebase AI Logic with the Gemini Developer API and the model rule in section 5. Features: a daily saving insight, GeminiChatDialog, GeminiChatViewModel (with resetUiState), and answers that are aware of the user's budgets, bills and spending.
What the user sees
• Home shows one insight sentence. Tap it for a short explanation and the numbers used. Chat opens from a button, not by default.
• Every AI answer leads with a 2-sentence summary; "Why this?" expands details.
• A small line states that AI output is informational help and not financial advice.
• If AI is unavailable, the insight falls back to a rule-based sentence computed from the user's own data (for example "Food spending is 22% above last month"). It must be true, and it must say nothing if there is nothing true to say.
What the model receives (data minimisation)
1. Send aggregates: category totals for the current and previous month, budget percentages, upcoming bill names and due dates. Do not send transaction notes, payee names, phone numbers, emails, UIDs or full transaction lists.
2. Never send roommate or household data that belongs to another person.
3. Treat all user-entered text (notes, category names) as untrusted. Pass it as data, never as instructions, and ignore instructions found in it (prompt injection).
4. AI features need a one-time opt-in consent screen in plain language, with an off switch in Settings. Check how Google's terms treat prompts for your chosen tier, and state the result in the privacy policy.
5. The model may not invent transactions or figures. When asked something the data cannot answer, it says so.
Reliability
• 30-second timeout, retry of transient errors with backoff, and correct cancellation.
• Friendly messages for no network, quota exceeded, App Check failure and "model not found" (which triggers the fallback model).
• Cache the daily insight and generate it at most once a day. Cap chat requests per user per day, and set a Gemini spending cap and budget alerts in the Firebase and Google Cloud consoles.
• Log only error categories to Crashlytics, never prompts or answers.
• Never disable App Check to make an AI call succeed.
10. Security, privacy and compliance
Authentication and phone ownership
• AuthManager supports Google (Credential Manager), email/password, and phone OTP. Firebase Auth is the identity authority; never trust a UID or verification flag sent by the client.
• Phone ownership runs in a callable function reservePhoneOwnership. The function reads the verified phone number from the caller's ID token, normalises it to E.164, and in one transaction writes phoneOwners/{hash} to the caller's uid. If another uid already owns the number, it refuses. Define behaviour for number change (release the old record), account deletion (release it), expiry and failure.
• PhoneValidation.kt and CountryPhoneInputField.kt: searchable country list, India (+91) default, national-digit extraction, E.164 output. PreferencesManager exposes isPhoneVerifiedFlow, getVerifiedPhoneFlow and getPhoneOwnerFlow; these mirror server state and never decide it.
App lock and privacy
• BiometricAuthManager, BiometricAuthScreen, AppLockMonitor using AndroidX Biometric with device-credential fallback. Configurable inactivity timeout; lock when the app goes to the background; clear session state on sign-out. Never simulate biometrics and never store face images.
• Hide balances in the recent-apps preview and block screenshots on sensitive screens (secure window flag), with a Settings toggle.
• LocalHideSensitiveAmounts is one central CompositionLocal backed by DataStore. It masks amounts on the dashboard, history, budgets, savings, roommate ledger, analytics, AI summaries, Dynamic Island and notification text.
App Check
Debug provider in development, Play Integrity in production, with separate configuration. Never ship debug tokens. Handle invalid-token errors with a clear message. Never turn App Check off to make a request pass.
Data protection under India's DPDP law
India's Digital Personal Data Protection Rules were notified on 13 November 2025 with phased start dates. Consent-manager registration begins around November 2026, and the main duties (notice, consent, security safeguards, breach reporting, deletion, children's data) start about 13 to 14 May 2027 (TaxGuru, Consent.in, AMSS regulatory alert). Confirm exact dates in the Gazette text before launch. Build these in now:
1. A plain-language privacy notice and consent screen before data is collected, with separate consent for AI processing and for contacts or notifications.
2. A consent log (what, when, which version) and a way to withdraw consent.
3. In-app account and data deletion, plus a data-export option. Google Play also expects an in-app account deletion path for apps that create accounts; confirm in the Play Data safety form.
4. A breach-response runbook. Reports describe a short notification deadline (72 hours is commonly cited); confirm in the Rules.
5. An 18-plus age declaration, because children's data triggers parental-consent duties.
6. Retention rules for each collection, written down.
7. Analytics events carry no amounts, names, phone numbers or notes.
Never claim end-to-end encryption. Say what is true: data is encrypted in transit, and Google Cloud encrypts Firestore at rest.
Permissions policy
Ask only for notifications (Android 13+ runtime permission), biometrics, and contacts only if a contact picker is built. No SMS read, call log, location or storage-wide access. Use the system file picker for CSV and the Photo Picker for receipts.
11. Signature experience and performance
The look is a premium, calm fintech interface: strong hierarchy, generous spacing, restrained gradients, meaningful empty states, and clear loading and error states. Essential-first rules from section 3 apply to every screen below.
Eight themes
Midnight Emerald (default), Cyber Violet, Solar Gold, Arctic Cyan, Crimson Pulse, Obsidian Rose, Aurora Borealis, Monochrome Slate. Each has a light and a dark mode and defines primary and secondary colours, backgrounds, surfaces, text, navigation, cards, dialogs, chart colours and component states. All live in Theme.kt. Selection is stored in DataStore, applies to the whole app at once, and survives restart. Check text contrast of at least 4.5:1 for body text in all 16 combinations, and never use colour alone to show meaning (add icons or labels for over-budget and negative values).
FinancialHero3DScene (Cash-Flow Reactor)
A Canvas-based interactive scene, not an image: an orbital core, touch-drag rotation, animated particle streams, income rings and expense rings, and live net-balance telemetry from real data.
• Disclosure role: this is a Level 0 visual. It shows only net position and the income/expense balance. Tap it to open the Level 1 breakdown. Amounts follow the masking setting.
• Performance: pre-allocate particle arrays and Paint/Path objects; no allocation inside the draw loop; drive animation from frame time; keep state reads inside the draw scope so the screen does not recompose each frame; stop animating when the app is in the background or the scene is off screen.
• Adaptive quality: measure frame time and reduce particle count and ring detail on slower devices. Honour the system "remove animations" setting by showing a still, readable version.
• Accessibility: a contentDescription states net balance, income and expenses in words (or "amounts hidden").
• Evidence: aim for the display's native refresh rate, including 120 Hz where supported. Measure with Macrobenchmark and JankStats, record frame-time results on at least one mid-range and one low-end device or emulator profile, and report the numbers. Do not claim a frame rate without a measurement.
DynamicIsland
A floating top capsule driven by real state: cash-flow health, active budget warnings, and quick actions. It shows one item at a time and only when there is something to act on ("Food budget at 85%", "Rent due tomorrow"). It animates smoothly, is reachable by screen readers, respects masking, and never shows invented or stale alerts. It handles display cutouts and small screens.
Layout and devices
• Call enableEdgeToEdge(). Apply Scaffold padding exactly once; handle status and navigation bar insets, display cutouts, keyboard overlap (imePadding), dialog clipping, text overflow and floating button collisions.
• Design first for a 360 dp wide phone with large font settings; then support tablets and foldables with window size classes.
• Choose the lowest minSdk that the verified dependencies allow (suggested target: API 26), because many users in India are on budget phones. Record the choice and the reason.
• Support Hindi and at least one more Indian language through string resources, with Indian digit grouping and the rupee symbol in every locale. Keep text expansion in mind for layouts.
12. Supporting features
Notifications
NotificationManager, SystemNotificationHelper, BillReminderWorker, BillActionReceiver. Five categories: Budget, Bill, Savings, Roommate, Insight, each with its own Android channel.
• A master switch and one switch per category. Turning the master off suppresses everything but keeps the category choices, so turning it back on restores them.
• Deduplicate by relatedId (for example budget id + threshold + month). Cancel or update a notification when its cause changes.
• Ask for the Android 13+ notification permission at the first moment it is useful, not at launch.
• WorkManager timing is approximate. Do not promise exact wall-clock delivery, and compute times in the user's time zone.
• The "Mark paid" action on a bill notification calls the same atomic markBillAsPaid path as the app.
• Respect masking: when amounts are hidden, notification text omits them.
• Limit volume: at most one notification per category per day unless the user acts; the daily insight is one notification.
CSV export and import
Export (exportToCsv): UTF-8, columns id, date (yyyy-MM-dd), type, category, amount (rupees, 2 decimals), note. Escape commas, quotes and line breaks. Neutralise formula injection: any text cell that starts with =, +, -, @, a tab or a carriage return is prefixed so spreadsheets read it as text. Numeric columns are exempt. Offer an option for UTF-8 with a byte-order mark so Excel shows Hindi text correctly.
Import (importFromCsv):
1. Pick the file with the system picker; set a sane size and row limit.
2. Validate required columns, amounts, dates (no future dates), types and categories.
3. Show a preview with counts: valid rows, rows with errors (with row numbers and reasons), and duplicates (within the file and against existing transactions).
4. Commit only after the user confirms, and only the valid, non-duplicate rows. Never import malformed rows silently.
5. Show a short summary afterwards.
